{
  "pattern_id": "e1ce3524",
  "name": "wasp_02_rule_grammar",
  "version": "1.0",
  "created": "2026-03-19T17:20:08.956176Z",
  "solution": {
    "brief": "Wasp rule grammar: multiple rules with directionality, controlling which connections can mate and in what orientation",
    "principle": "\n\nRelated insight: Related insight: Tutorial 03 (wasp_03_graph_grammar) extends the rule grammar concept introduced here by adding a predefined graph structure that drives aggregation. While tutorial 02 teaches which connections can mate and in what orientation (stochastic selection), tutorial 03 introduces graph grammar where the growth sequence and topology are constrained by a predefined graph — enabling deterministic, reproducible assemblies rather than random growth. The rule grammar defined here is a prerequisite to understanding graph-grammar-driven aggregation.\n\nRelated insight: Related insight: Field-driven aggregation (wasp_04_field_driven) adds a complementary control layer to the rule grammar established here. While rule grammar governs which connections can mate and in what orientation, scalar fields (attractors, density gradients) bias which rules fire and where parts are placed — combining rule-based connection compatibility with field-based spatial steering. The rule grammar defined in this tutorial is a prerequisite for field-driven setups, as fields select among valid rule-compatible placements rather than replacing rule logic.\n\nRelated insight: Related insight: The advanced RuleGenerator series (tutorials 11–12, wasp_11 and wasp_12) builds on the rule grammar concepts established here, extending bidirectional rule definitions and connection-mating grammar into a generative rule-generator workflow for complex multi-part assemblies. The rule grammar fundamentals in this tutorial are a prerequisite for understanding the RuleGenerator-based aggregation patterns in the advanced series.\n\nRelated insight: Related insight: Tutorial 13 (wasp_13) further extends the RuleGenerator series beyond tutorials 11 and 12, continuing the advanced multi-part aggregation workflow. The rule grammar fundamentals established in this tutorial (tutorial 02) are a prerequisite for the entire RuleGenerator series (tutorials 11–13).",
    "components_needed": [
